            {
              "id": "63683132-4a0d-4a3c-a5d4-755e49683144",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": "<em><strong>Ambient Play</strong></em> was written by Larissa Hjorth &amp; Ingrid Richardson, published by The MIT Press, September 15, 2020."
            },
            {
              "id": "73453b3c-6bd1-4daf-8b9a-0618f64cedb1",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": ""
            },
            {
              "id": "d059e1a6-0437-480a-81ae-9bb5cd0f1821",
              "type": "quote",
              "html": "<strong><em>An engaging look at how </em></strong><a href=\"#/mobile-gaming\"><strong><em>mobile games</em></strong></a><strong><em> are increasingly part of our day-to-day lives and the ways that we interact across real as well as digital landscapes.<br></em></strong><em><br>We often play games on our mobile devices when we have some time to kill--waiting in line, pausing between tasks, stuck on a bus. We play in solitude or in company, alone in a bedroom or with others in the family room. In </em>Ambient Play<em>, Larissa Hjorth and Ingrid Richardson examine how mobile gameplay fits into our day-to-day lives. They show that as mobile games spread across different </em><a href=\"#/genre\"><em>genres</em></a><em>, </em><a href=\"#/hardware\"><em>platforms</em></a><em>, practices, and contexts, they become an important way of experiencing and navigating a digitally saturated world. We are digital wayfarers, moving constantly among digital, social, and social worlds.</em>"
            },
            {
              "id": "55437f25-a44c-4728-99fd-4bb1ae5b65f0",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": ""
            }
          ]
        },
